Objective

Master the foundational 20% of Kubernetes / K3s operations that account for 80% of day-to-day deployment, verification, debugging, and teardown tasks.

Commands Executed & Workflow

1. Cluster Health Check

Verified that the K3s cluster nodes and core system components are up, running, and healthy.

k3s kubectl get nodes
k3s kubectl get pods -A


Key Learning: Quickly check if the control-plane and system services (like CoreDNS, Traefik, and metrics-server) are running smoothly across all namespaces.

2. Workload Deployment & Network Exposure

Deployed an Nginx test container and exposed it via an internal ClusterIP service.

k3s kubectl run mamba-web --image=nginx --port=80
k3s kubectl expose pod mamba-web --type=ClusterIP --port=80


Key Learning: How to spin up rapid test pods and attach a service routing layer inside the cluster. (Note: Avoid spaces around = characters when passing flags like --port=80).

3. Verification of Resources

Checked active pods and services in the default namespace.

k3s kubectl get pods,svc


Key Learning: Confirming that both the application pod and its corresponding ClusterIP service are active and properly mapped.

4. Debugging & Log Inspection

Pulled container runtime logs from the active Nginx pod to inspect startup sequences and configuration details.

k3s kubectl logs mamba-web


Key Learning: Essential for troubleshooting misconfigured containers, missing entrypoints, or runtime errors.

5. Teardown & Environment Sanitation

Cleaned up the cluster by deleting the test pod and service, leaving the default namespace spotless.

k3s kubectl delete pod mamba-web && k3s kubectl delete svc mamba-web
k3s kubectl get pods,svc


Key Learning: Ensuring no ghost resources or stale configurations are left behind in the cluster.
